From 69267d6a053491da6cac05770e718cc856716421 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:40:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 13/19] wifi: rtl8192f-soc: install pairwise CCMP keys in the
 security CAM

The MAC has a hardware security engine - 64 CAM entries reached only
through the indirect command/data pair at 0x670/0x674/0x678 - and the
shipped firmware uses it by default (SWCRYPTO is off unless a MIB turns
it on, 8192cd_ioctl.c 1288).  Our .set_key returned 1 for every key, so
all encryption ran in the host for a part that can do it in silicon.

Program a CAM entry for pairwise CCMP and leave everything else in
software:

 - entry layout is CAMProgramEntry88XX() (Hal88XXGen.c 194-233): dword 0
   holds address bytes 0 and 1 in bits[31:16] plus the config word,
   dword 1 the remaining address bytes, dwords 2..5 the 16 key bytes
   little-endian per dword, and the six content dwords go in top-down so
   the word carrying CAM_CFG_VALID - bit15, the same bit
   CAMFindUsable88XX() tests to decide whether an entry is free - is the
   last one to land;
 - the config word is what CamAddOneEntry() builds: CFG_VALID |
   algorithm << 2 | key id (8192cd_hw.c 19245, algorithm values from
   8192cd_security.h 116-124 passed pre-shifted at 8192cd_net80211.c
   1064);
 - the engine is switched on with the first entry, as the vendor does:
   REG_CR bit9 (BIT_MAC_SEC_EN) plus REG_SECCFG TXENC | RXDEC | NOSKMC |
   CHK_KEYID (8192cd_hw.c 19273-19283, with the SCO_* to BIT_* mapping
   in HalComBit.h 18224-18230);
 - pairwise keys are allocated from entry 4 upwards so entries 0..3 stay
   free for the key-id-indexed group search, for the same reason rtw88
   gives in rtw_sec_get_free_cam();
 - group keys stay in software, which is the vendor's own default on
   this part (set_gkey_to_cam = 0, 8192cd_security.c 621) and is what
   NOSKMC enforces on receive; TKIP, WEP and any key length other than
   16 bytes keep returning 1, and a full CAM falls back to software
   rather than evicting another peer.

On the data path the TX descriptor names the algorithm for the engine
(TX_DW1_SECTYPE, HalComTXDesc.h 117/132/522-525) and carries no key
index, because the engine selects the entry by the frame's address -
which is why set_key stores the peer address alongside the key.  The
engine writes the CCMP header itself, so the key asks mac80211 for
PUT_IV_SPACE plus RESERVE_TAILROOM; the vendor reserves the same room by
lengthening the header segment of any hardware-encrypted frame: "CCMP:
1) iv is CCMP header, 2) mic for hw encrypt" (SetTxBufferDesc88XX(),
Hal88XXTxDesc.c 1452-1460).  Without those flags the association
completed but nothing the AP sent was decodable: the client's own
frames reached the AP and were decrypted by the hardware, while frames
sourced by the AP never appeared at the peer at all.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
